¶ditem©dbodyy≥levi's core premise is status resolved against git ancestry, and GitHub's most common merge strategy silently breaks it: a squash merge replaces the branch commit with a new one, so a close anchored at the branch commit is never an ancestor of main and the task reads OPEN forever after the fix has shipped.

Hit twice in one session (2026-07-21): PR #9's close anchored at 6369241c, squashed to e9dafa9 ‚Äî lv-ef44 read open on main with the fix live in production. Rebasing before merge does the same thing (lv-7fa4, anchored pre-rebase at 3a6d8639). Both needed manual reopen + close --anchor <new sha>.

levi already has patch-id rewritten-anchor detection (see cli_git_sync.rs rewritten_anchor_suggests_the_new_sha) but it only fires when the anchor is unreachable from ANY ref. After a squash merge the original usually IS still reachable from the local branch, so levi reports the softer 'fix exists elsewhere ‚Äî consider merging it' instead, which is misleading once the squash has already landed on main.

